course: everyday
lesson: 244
title: Building and making things safely
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Building is how engineers think: plan, follow the steps, test, and when something fails, change one thing and try again. Failures are how you learn.
explain: Real tools need real care. Hold a nail low and start with light taps, let an adult help with a hot glue gun, and work with paint and glue where fresh air can flow. A splinter should come out with clean tweezers so it does not get infected.
explain: Things you build must not fall on anyone. A blanket den should use light things; a treehouse needs an adult, strong wood and a safe height.
explain: Sand and snow can collapse. Holes in sand deeper than your knees and tunnels in sand or snow can fall in and bury a child, and snowploughs cannot see a fort in a snowbank by the road.
explain: Example: a girl in Wellington builds a sandcastle. Dry sand crumbles, so she wets it, and it holds its shape. She keeps her hole shallow and does not dig a tunnel under the dune.
fact: Clear Lego bricks off the floor into a box after playing, because stepping on them hurts and pieces get lost.
fact: Follow building instructions one step at a time and check before going on, because skipped steps lead to mistakes.
fact: Make blanket dens with light things that will not fall, and tidy up after, because heavy furniture can tip over.
fact: Build a treehouse or outdoor fort with an adult, checking the wood is strong and the height is safe, because falls cause broken bones.
fact: When hammering, hold the nail low, start with light taps and keep fingers away.
fact: If you hurt yourself and it bleeds a lot, press a clean cloth on it and get an adult.
fact: Ask an adult to take out a splinter with clean tweezers, because a splinter left in can get infected.
fact: Let an adult help with a hot glue gun, and never touch the nozzle or the hot glue, because it burns skin.
fact: Use model paint and glue in a ventilated place (with fresh air flowing through) and follow the label, because fumes can make you feel ill.
fact: Wet sand holds its shape better than dry sand; never throw sand, because it hurts and scratches eyes.
fact: Do not dig holes in sand deeper than your knees or tunnel into the sand, because they can collapse and bury a child.
fact: Ask before using a cardboard box for a project, and recycle it after.
fact: Never dig deep tunnels in snow banks, especially near roads, because snow can collapse and ploughs cannot see you.
fact: When a project does not work first time, change one thing, test and try again; changing only one thing at a time shows which change made the difference, and engineers learn from failures.
exercise: You finish building with Lego on the living-room floor. What should you do? | Put the bricks back in the box | Leave them for tomorrow's game | Push them under the sofa | Leave just the small ones out
exercise: You are building a model from instructions. What is the best way? | One step at a time, checking before going on | Look at the picture on the box and skip straight to the end | Do the steps in any order you like | Do several steps all at once, so that you save some time
exercise: You make a den with blankets. What should hold the blankets up? | Light chairs and cushions that will not fall on anyone | A heavy bookcase pulled out into the middle of the room | The TV stand on its side | A tall lamp leaning against the sofa
exercise: You want to build a treehouse. What is the safe way? | With an adult, strong wood and a safe height | Alone, as high up in the tree as you can possibly get | With old thin planks you found in the shed | On the thinnest branches, so it sways
exercise: You are hammering a nail with your dad. How do you hold it? | Low down, with light taps at first | At the very top, with big hard swings | Between your teeth, so both hands are free | Without holding it, and hit very hard
exercise: A splinter is in your hand after playing with wood. What do you do? | Ask an adult to remove it with clean tweezers | Leave it, because it will come out on its own | Dig it out with a dirty pin | Push it in deeper, so that it stops hurting you
exercise: Why do you need an adult with a hot glue gun? | The nozzle and the glue are hot enough to burn | Glue guns can explode if you shake them about | The glue is poisonous to touch even when it is cold | Glue guns only work for adults
exercise: Where should you paint and glue your model plane? | In a room with fresh air, following the label | In a small cupboard with the door shut tight | Under your blanket in bed | In the car with all the windows wound up so the dust stays out
exercise: Your sandcastle keeps falling apart. What will help? | Use wet sand, which holds its shape | Use the driest sand you can possibly find | Build it much higher and thinner | Pat it all over with a dry towel
exercise: Your brother throws sand in a game. Why is that wrong? | Sand in the eyes hurts and scratches them | Sand makes hair turn white | It wastes the sand on the beach for other people | Sand is only meant for building castles
exercise: How deep can you safely dig a hole in the sand? | No deeper than your knees, with no tunnels | As deep as you are tall, so you can stand in it | Deep enough to climb right in and hide from everyone | Any depth at all, because sand never falls in
exercise: You want to use the big box in the garage for a rocket. What do you do first? | Ask whether anyone needs it | Cut it up straight away before anyone asks | Take it, because boxes are rubbish | Take it and hide it in your room
exercise: Your friend wants to dig a snow cave in the bank by the road. What do you say? | No, it can collapse and ploughs cannot see us | Yes, a road bank is the perfect place for a cave | Yes, if we dig really deep | Yes, snow is far too light and soft to hurt anyone
exercise: Your paper bridge falls down when you test it. What should you do? | Change one thing and test it again | Give up, because it will never work | Blame the paper and stop trying for good | Build exactly the same bridge again
test: A boy in Florida digs a hole on the beach until he can stand in it up to his chest. Why is it dangerous? | The sand walls can collapse and bury him | He might find a crab at the bottom | The hole will fill up with fresh water from under the sand | The sand at the bottom will get much too hot to stand on
test: In a snowy town in Minnesota, why must children not build forts in the snow piled by the road? | The snow can collapse, and ploughs cannot see them | The snow by roads is far too dirty and salty to build with | The forts melt faster near roads | Cars need that snow to help them slow down at the corners
test: Your den is held up by a tall bookcase you tugged over. What is the danger? | Heavy furniture can tip over and hurt someone | The bookcase will get dusty | The books might get creased and bent | Blankets are much too heavy for bookcases to hold up
test: A girl touches the hot glue she has just squeezed out. What will probably happen? | She will burn her finger | The glue will feel cool and wet | Nothing, glue is never hot | Her finger will stick forever
test: Why do engineers change only one thing at a time when testing? | So they can tell which change made the difference | Because changing more than one thing is against the rules | So the test takes longer | Because one change always fixes it
test: A boy builds a model in a closed bedroom and gets a headache from the paint smell. What should he have done? | Worked where fresh air could flow, following the label | Used more paint so that he could finish quicker | Closed the window to keep warm | Held his breath the whole time he was painting
test: Which nail-hammering habit keeps fingers safest? | Light taps first, then fingers away once it stands | Holding the nail at the very top | Hitting as hard as possible right from the very start | Holding the nail with your whole hand around it
test: Why should a splinter not be left in your skin for days? | It can get infected | It will grow into a thorn | It turns into wood under your skin | It makes your hand smell
test: building the lego set, skipped step 12, it's prob fine | Go back, follow it one step at a time and check | Keep going, you'll fix it all at the end | Throw away the instructions | Use glue to make it stay together
test: got a splinter, gonna dig it out with a pin | Ask an adult to take it out with clean tweezers | Leave it in, it falls out by itself | Squeeze it hard with your dirty fingers till it pops | Bite it out
test: making a den, can i use the big bookcase as a wall | No, use light things that won't tip over | Yes, heavy things make the strongest walls | Yes, if you push it hard against the sofa | Only if you climb on top to hold it still
test: robot car won't move, tried it once, it's broken forever | Change one thing, test and try again | Give up, it will never work now | Change everything at once | Hit it until it moves
explain: A boy in Ohio used his dad's power drill without asking; it jumped off a screw and went into his hand, and he needed surgery.
explain: A girl in England called Ruby built a den roof from old planks and stood on it; it gave way, she fell two metres and broke her wrist.
fact: Power tools can cause deep cuts and lost fingers in a second, so children use them only with an adult right there.
fact: Things you build may not hold your weight, so an adult checks them before anyone climbs or stands on them.
test: dad's drill is in the shed, can i use it while he's out | No, only with an adult right there | Yes, if you go slowly | Yes, if you hold it with both hands | Only for small holes
test: Why did Ruby's den roof give way under her? | It was not strong enough for her weight | Den roofs always break in the rain | She built it too pretty | It was made from the wrong colour of wood
test: hammered my thumb n it's bleeding loads | Press a clean cloth on it and get an adult | Keep hammering, it'll stop on its own | Wash it in the paint water | Hide it so you don't get told off
test: did u hear a kid built a den with a big bookcase and it tipped over on him? real lesson? | Build dens with light things that won't fall | Use heavy furniture so the den is nice and strong | Only build dens in the garden | Bookcases never tip over
test: apparently some boys built a treehouse with no adult and the floor broke and one broke his leg. what's the true lesson? | Build with an adult who checks the wood and height | Build it higher up in the tree so it's safer | Use more nails and it'll be fine | Treehouses never break
test: my mate says his cousin lost a finger to a saw in like one second. probably blown up? | Maybe, but power tools really can do that; adults only | All made up, saws are slow | Saws can't cut fingers | Only electric toothbrushes are dangerous
exam: Identify the appropriate response when a project does not succeed at the first attempt. | One thing is altered, tested and tried again | The project is abandoned at once | Everything is changed simultaneously | The instructions are discarded entirely
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a splinter left in the skin? | It may become infected and is removed by an adult | It dissolves harmlessly within a day | It must be cut out with a knife | It is best left in the skin for a week or so
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice regarding power tools. | Children use them only with an adult present | Children may use them alone once shown how | Children may use them in the garden | Children may use them alone provided that they wear gloves
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an adult checks a structure before anyone climbs upon it. | It may not hold a person's weight | Adults must sign the structure | It needs to be painted first | Climbing is forbidden without a ticket
